WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.900
Input sources in JavaScript span both the browser and the server.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.375
In the browser, visible form fields are only the beginning.

00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:10.586
Query strings, route parameters, hidden fields, cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage,

00:00:10.586 --> 00:00:15.700
postMessage events, file uploads, drag-and-drop data, pasted content, browser extensions,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.118
and responses from APIs can all influence application behavior.

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:22.270
A value being present in the browser does not make it trustworthy.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.680
On the server side, Node.js applications commonly process request bodies, headers,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.315
cookies, path parameters, query parameters, JSON APIs, GraphQL operations,

00:00:31.315 --> 00:00:35.385
WebSocket messages, uploaded files, queue messages, scheduled job data,

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:37.670
configuration files, and environment variables.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.540
Some of these inputs may feel internal, but they can still be malformed, stale,

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.900
compromised, or outside the current application contract. Storage values require caution.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.200
Data in localStorage or sessionStorage can be modified by the user or by scripts running

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.500
in the page. Cookies can be changed unless they are protected and verified appropriately.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.980
Database records may have originated from older versions of the application,

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.370
partner feeds, imports, or compromised accounts.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.610
Validation should focus on trust boundaries,

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.100
not only on whether a value came from a public form.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.300
A practical first step is to map where data enters the application and where it goes next.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.715
For each input source, identify who controls it, how it is parsed,

00:01:12.715 --> 00:01:16.900
what type and shape are expected, what business rules apply, and which code consumes it.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.900
That map helps developers choose the right validation point instead of scattering checks

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.233
randomly across the codebase.
